abstract: We give a source-resolved spectral criterion for long-range tensor exchange between
  conserved matter sources. Exact channel elimination preserves the propagation kernel together
  with the source and readout maps and the direct response. Common-space estimates control the
  residue at the displaced critical root of a finite approximation. A positive Källén–Lehmann
  representation identifies this residue with a zero-invariant-mass atom even at a continuum
  endpoint. For a complete positive Lorentz-covariant conserved stress tensor we classify the
  null-shell measure and prove that its TT atomic weight vanishes. A reduced compact spectral
  realisation instead carries internal tensor vertices into an external two-helicity response.
  Conservation reduces the leading vertex of localised stationary matter to its total energy.
  The mixed residue then factorises into the two energies and a common propagation coefficient.
  Spatial resolvent estimates determine the potential and force with finite-size corrections.
  Normalisation through the matter vertex leaves one dimensional calibration for the overall
  Planck scale. The spectral residue fixes the dimensionless exchange strength. The resulting
  response admits an Einstein–matter effective description under the stated positivity and helicity
  assumptions together with universal soft coupling and local nonlinear consistency.
